SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Free Primary Schooling in the DRC: Progress Despite the Perils","publisher":"World Bank Blogs","publication_date":"2022-06-27","url":"https://blogs.worldbank.org/en/nasikiliza/free-primary-schooling-drc-progress-despite-perils","claim":"The DRC's free-primary-schooling reform had expanded access by June 2022, with the World Bank saying enrollment had risen by about 3 million children and the nationwide grievance hotline 'Allo Ecole' was functional.","locator":"lines 48-60","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary institutional blog by World Bank staff, directly dated and describing implementation details and outputs in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 execution of a major government social-spending reform that began in 2019 but was still being implemented in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Strong but not exclusive: the reform is a Tshisekedi-administration policy, with World Bank co-financing and implementation support.","contrary_evidence":["The same source notes teacher strikes, salary-payment problems, and fraud findings in the education sector."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Free Primary Schooling in the DRC: Progress Despite the Perils","publisher":"World Bank Blogs","publication_date":"2022-06-27","url":"https://blogs.worldbank.org/en/nasikiliza/free-primary-schooling-drc-progress-despite-perils","claim":"By June 2022, the education project had disbursed $197 million, over 180,000 codes of conduct had been signed, and more than 36,000 primary teachers had been placed on the public payroll.","locator":"lines 50-54","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same institutional source, with concrete implementation metrics and third-party verification language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 implementation progress under the target ruler-period.","ruler_attribution":"Strong but not exclusive: the outputs are tied to the government program under Tshisekedi, though delivered through a donor-supported project.","contrary_evidence":["The source explicitly says the reform remains a work in progress and still faces insecurity and technical constraints."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Strengthening the Social Contract: Working towards safer and more inclusive schooling in Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/opinion/2022/06/08/strengthening-the-social-contract-working-towards-safer-and-more-inclusive-schooling-in-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The World Bank said DRC's grievance-redress approach around free primary education had expanded enrollment to 3 million more children and made the system more accountable, with the 'Allo Ecole' hotline operating nationwide and receiving 1,200 complaints in its first week.","locator":"lines 53-60","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct dated World Bank commentary with specific implementation detail and measurable service-delivery output.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period evidence for 2022 social-service implementation and feedback mechanisms.","ruler_attribution":"Strong but partly mediated by donor support; still relevant as a government-led reform under Tshisekedi.","contrary_evidence":["The same article emphasizes hidden costs, complaints, and ongoing concerns about teacher pay and abuse redress."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of Congo Economic Update: Harnessing Growth Through Digital Connectivity to Create Jobs and Reduce Poverty","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/07/democratic-republic-of-congo-economic-update","claim":"The World Bank said the government had liberalized the telecommunications sector by publishing a new Telecommunications Law, but secondary legislation, fiber-backbone development, PPPs, and tax simplification were still needed to operationalize the reform.","locator":"lines 145-147","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct institutional assessment of a 2022 policy change and its next implementation steps.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period for 2022 policy design and implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this reflects an administration-backed regulatory reform, though execution depends on ministers and regulators beyond the president alone.","contrary_evidence":["The same source says major challenges remained and that the law still required secondary legislation to become fully operational."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of Congo Economic Update: Harnessing Growth Through Digital Connectivity to Create Jobs and Reduce Poverty","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/07/democratic-republic-of-congo-economic-update","claim":"The same World Bank update estimated that a 10% increase in broadband penetration could add about 2.5% to GDP and create close to 700,000 jobs.","locator":"lines 141-143","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Quantified policy pathway from a major multilateral institution with a clearly dated 2022 outlook.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The estimate is a 2022 policy/outcome projection, not a realized outcome, but it is still relevant to the target-year economic strategy.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: this is an external model, not a ruler action, but it speaks to the administration's chosen digital-growth path.","contrary_evidence":["It is a projection, not observed 2022 realization."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Empowering Women Entrepreneurs to Boost Economic Transformation in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-05-02","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/05/02/empowering-women-entrepreneurs-to-boost-economic-transformation-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The World Bank approved $300 million for the TRANSFORME DRC project to support women entrepreneurs, create up to 2,500 formal firms, expand access to finance for 5,000 MSMEs, and contribute to a projected 28,000 jobs over five years.","locator":"lines 146-149","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct project approval with explicit targets, sectors, and supervisory ministry named.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and tied to the ruler-period's private-sector policy agenda.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to strong: the project is government-supervised and aligned to Tshisekedi-era diversification policy, though results depend on implementation and donors.","contrary_evidence":["The source gives projected outputs, not achieved 2022 outcomes."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo | EITI","publisher":"EITI","publication_date":"2022-12-13","url":"https://eiti.org/countries/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"The DRC's beneficial-ownership disclosure regime was still only partly met in 2022: there was no public beneficial-ownership registry and the legal framework for reporting was still incomplete.","locator":"lines 571-576","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"EITI validation summary is an authoritative, dated assessment of implementation quality and gaps.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the target year and the extractive-governance environment relevant to 2022 economic stewardship.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the gap reflects government-level failure to fully operationalize transparency rules under the Tshisekedi administration.","contrary_evidence":["The same EITI page also records systematic disclosure improvements and a higher transparency score in other extractive-data areas."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo | EITI","publisher":"EITI","publication_date":"2022-12-13","url":"https://eiti.org/countries/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"In the 2018-2020 EITI reporting cycle, only 21 of 77 significant extractive licensees submitted beneficial-ownership information, showing that implementation remained incomplete at the end of 2022.","locator":"lines 576-578","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same authoritative validation record, with a concrete implementation statistic and clear period fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Still relevant to 2022 because it evaluates the live state of the system at year-end.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is a government-regulatory and disclosure failure under Tshisekedi's administration, though not a single personal decision.","contrary_evidence":["The same EITI profile notes that additional data collection later produced open-format disclosures for 56 companies."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Providing safety nets for the most vulnerable in the Democratic Republic of Congo: Incremental steps with a large footprint","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-02-10","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2023/02/10/providing-safety-nets-for-the-most-vulnerable-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo-incremental-steps-with-a-large-footpri","claim":"By December 2022, the STEP social-protection program had improved access to livelihoods for 530,000 beneficiaries, including more than 200,000 women, and upgraded 2,185 community infrastructures such as schools and clinics.","locator":"results section, lines 94-95","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Result note from the World Bank with a clear end-2022 outcome statement and specific beneficiary counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2023 but explicitly reports outcomes through December 2022, so the temporal fit is good for the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is a donor-backed safety-net program, but it reflects the broader administration's social-protection environment and implementation capacity.","contrary_evidence":["The result page is retrospective and donor-mediated, so it does not isolate direct presidential causation."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Children’s education, a priority for Marlène","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/recits/childrens-education-a-priority-for-Marlene","claim":"UNICEF reported that more than 4,000 vulnerable families in N'Sele received unconditional cash transfers that helped them pay school costs and improve access to basic social services.","locator":"lines 80-86","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF provides a concrete beneficiary example from a documented cash-transfer program, though the page is a human-interest story rather than an audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and tied to household-level welfare support in the ruler-period.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the intervention is donor-backed and local, but it evidences delivery of a broader social-protection and education-support environment under Tshisekedi.","contrary_evidence":["This is a localized intervention and does not prove national-scale distributional success."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rapport Annuel 2022","publisher":"Banque Centrale du Congo","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.bcc.cd/publications/rapports-annuels/rapport-annuel-2022","claim":"The central bank's 2022 annual report said its priorities were to strengthen confidence in the Congolese franc, reduce dependence on foreign currency in domestic transactions, digitize payments, automate controls, and use monetary policy as a lever for inclusive development and social protection.","locator":"lines 635-638","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official annual-report foreword from the central bank governor, dated to the target year, with explicit policy orientation but not audited outcomes.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly describes 2022 monetary-policy orientation.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: the BCC is institutionally separate, so this is better read as administration-level macro governance than direct presidential control.","contrary_evidence":["The passage states priorities, not realized outcomes, and does not by itself show successful stabilization."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}

**Ledger**
- Candidates found: 46
- Documents opened: 23
- Duplicate families collapsed: 17
- Irrelevant or out-of-period: 7
- Inaccessible: 3
- Accepted source-claim units: 11
- Accepted distinct URLs: 8

**Gaps**
- Direct 2022 budget-execution and audited-outcome evidence is still thinner than the planning-and-project paper trail.
- Several strong items are donor-backed or multilateral, so ruler attribution is usually moderate rather than exclusive.
- I still lack a clean primary record tying specific macroeconomic outcomes to Tshisekedi personally rather than to the finance ministry, central bank, or external shocks.
- IMF eLibrary pages returned 403, so the best next step is alternate IMF mirrors/PDFs or partner summaries with the same report text.
- Better next sources would be the 2022 budget execution report, Court of Accounts/IGF releases, BCC PDF report text, and any 2022 Ministry of Finance or telecom-law implementing decrees.

No score assigned.
